    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Prostate cancers exhibit a range of clinical behaviors, from indolent to highly aggressive, reflecting differences in underlying biology. Tailored treatments are needed to match those differences. Our lab previously identified a subset of prostate cancers marked by expression of Alpha-2-Glycoprotein 1, Zinc-Binding, (AZGP1) that is associated with more favorable patient outcomes. AZGP1 is also expressed in mature prostatic secretory epithelium, and we have speculated that it marks a particular state of prostatic epithelial differentiation where the corresponding cancerous cells are clinically less aggressive. Here, we have investigated the connection between AZGP1and prostatic epithelial differentiation using a three dimensional (3D) cell culture model system. We show that prostatic epithelial cells grown in a basement membrane rich extract form spheroid cell aggregates that exhibit morphologic differentiation, and that AZGP1 expression is induced in a subset of cells coincident with morphologic differentiation. To investigate a functional link between AZGP1 and prostatic epithelial differentiation, we used CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing to knock out AZGP1 in prostatic epithelial cells. Notably, AZGP1 knockout disrupts normal morphologic differentiation, producing spheroids with increased pyknotic and fragmented nuclei. By transcriptome profiling, the AZGP1 knockout spheroids also exhibit lost induction of genes associated with epithelial differentiation, and lost repression of genes of the mitotic cell cycle. Finally, the genes regulated by AZGP1 in 3D culture show enrichment among the genes co-expressed with AZGP1 in normal prostate tissue, validating our 3D model system. Taken together, our findings reveal that AZGP1 not only marks prostatic epithelial differentiation, but likely functions as a master regulator of cell fate, inducing a molecular differentiation program while repressing cell division. Agents that induce or mimic AZGP1 activity may provide novel therapies for prostate cancer.",
